    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d140">

                <pb facs="0178" n="168" sameAs="frus:frus1947v07/pg168"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/6–647</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Director of the Office of Far Eastern
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Vincent</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1947-06-06">June 6, 1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I believe you will find it worthwhile reading the attached brief<anchor n="46"
                        xml:id="d140-f1"/> of Ambassador Stuart’s recent report<anchor n="47"
                        xml:id="d140-f2"/> on the situation in China. He makes no recommendations
                    and indulges in no predictions other than to state in his final paragraph that
                    “mob violence may break out” but that “apart from this there is not apt to be a
                    collapse so much as disintegration”. However, he does say that “our policy may
                    have <pb facs="0179" n="169" xml:id="pg169"/> a determining influence” by which
                    he means, I gather, our policy with regard to aid. My own thought is that any
                    aid which it would be practicable to give China now can at best have only a
                    moderating influence.</p>
                <p>We are moving forward now with a program for the “limited” type of military and
                    financial aid in accordance with your instructions of last week.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>J[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ohn</hi>] C[<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >arter</hi>] V[<hi rendition="#small-caps">incent</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
